  "kind": "theory",
  "topic": "Interaction principles 2–5: equilibrium, efficiency vs equity, markets and government",
  "explain": "<p>These 4 slides are interaction principles 2, 3, 4 and 5 (of the 5 on slide 33). One principle per slide.</p><p><b>Slide 48: Markets move toward equilibrium.</b> The definition: a situation is in <b>equilibrium</b> when <i>no individual would be better off doing something different</i>. The picture is supermarket checkout lines. Think about it: if one line is short, people move to it. Soon all lines are about the same length. Now nobody gains by switching lines. That is equilibrium. Nobody wants to change what they do.</p><p><b>Slide 49: Resources should be used as efficiently as possible to achieve society's goals.</b> Two words:</p><ul><li><b>Efficient:</b> an economy is efficient if it takes <i>all</i> opportunities to make some people better off <i>without making other people worse off</i>. Tiny example: a free seat in class while a friend is standing. Giving him the seat helps him and hurts nobody. If nobody does it, that is <i>not</i> efficient (a chance is wasted).</li><li><b>Equity:</b> everyone gets his or her <i>fair share</i>. But people disagree about what is \"fair\". So equity is <b>not as well-defined</b> as efficiency.</li></ul><p><b>Slide 50: Markets usually lead to efficiency.</b> The <b>incentives</b> in a market economy already make sure resources are usually put <b>to good use</b>, and chances to make people better off are <b>not wasted</b>. (Sellers want profit, buyers want a good deal, so good trades happen by themselves.)</p><p><b>Slide 51: When markets don't achieve efficiency, government intervention can improve society's welfare.</b> The slide gives 3 cases where the market fails:</p><ol><li><b>Side effects:</b> people's actions have side effects that the market does not count properly (the textbook calls these externalities). Example: a factory pollutes the air of people who are not part of the trade.</li><li><b>Monopoly:</b> one party blocks good (mutually beneficial) trades to grab a bigger share of resources for itself.</li><li><b>Goods unsuited for markets:</b> some goods, by their nature, cannot be managed well by markets. Slide example: <b>drugs</b>.</li></ol>",
  "question": "<p>Two questions are likely from these slides:</p><p><b>Q1.</b> What is equilibrium? Explain it using the supermarket checkout line example. (Principle: markets move toward equilibrium.)</p><p><b>Q2.</b> Define efficiency and equity. Why is equity less well-defined than efficiency? Markets usually lead to efficiency; when they do not, how can government help? Give the three cases.</p><p>(It could also be asked as one item: \"Explain interaction principles 2–5.\")</p>",
  "answer": "<p><b>Q1.</b> An economic situation is in <b>equilibrium</b> when no individual would be better off doing something different. Markets move toward equilibrium. Example: at a supermarket, if one checkout line is shorter, shoppers move to it. The short line gets longer and the long lines get shorter, until all lines are about equal. Then no shopper can gain by switching lines, so nobody changes. This is equilibrium.</p><p><b>Q2.</b></p><ul><li><b>Efficiency:</b> an economy is efficient if it takes all opportunities to make some people better off without making other people worse off.</li><li><b>Equity:</b> everyone gets his or her fair share.</li><li>Equity is less well-defined because people disagree about what is \"fair\". Efficiency has a clear test (is any chance to help someone, without hurting others, left unused?).</li><li>Resources should be used as efficiently as possible to achieve society's goals.</li><li><b>Markets usually lead to efficiency:</b> the incentives built into a market economy make sure resources are usually put to good use and opportunities to make people better off are not wasted.</li><li><b>When markets don't achieve efficiency, government intervention can improve society's welfare.</b> This happens when:<ol><li>individual actions have <i>side effects</i> that the market does not take into account properly (e.g. pollution);</li><li>one party prevents mutually beneficial trades to capture a greater share of resources for itself (e.g. <b>monopoly</b>);</li><li>some goods, by their very nature, are unsuited for efficient management by markets (e.g. <b>drugs</b>).</li></ol></li></ul>",
