  "explain": "<p>Slide 12 says economics is a <b>scientific study of the economy</b>. Then it lists the questions economics tries to answer. There are 5 groups:</p><ul><li><b>Jobs:</b> Who creates jobs? What kinds of jobs? How many jobs are created?</li><li><b>Production:</b> What to produce? How much? How to produce?</li><li><b>Distribution:</b> Who gets it, and how much?</li><li><b>Consumption:</b> Consume what? How much?</li><li><b>Rich vs poor:</b> Why are some countries rich and other countries poor?</li></ul><p>The last line (in red on the slide) is the key point: <b>all these questions are based on a set of common principles</b>. These common principles are the <b>12 economic principles</b> of the course: 4 on individual choice + 5 on interaction + 3 on economy-wide interactions.</p><p>Everyday example: a bakery. What to bake (bread or cakes)? How many? By hand or with a machine? Who gets the bread (whoever pays)? How many workers does it hire? Every one of these is an economics question.</p>",
  "question": "<p>Economics is the scientific study of the economy. What kinds of questions does economics try to answer? List them, and say what they all have in common.</p>",
  "answer": "<p>Economics is the scientific study of the economy. It asks questions about:</p><ol><li><b>Jobs</b>: who creates jobs, what kinds of jobs, and how many jobs are created.</li><li><b>Production</b>: what to produce, how much to produce, and how to produce it.</li><li><b>Distribution</b>: who gets the goods, and how much each gets.</li><li><b>Consumption</b>: what to consume and how much.</li><li><b>Wealth of nations</b>: why some countries are rich and other countries are poor.</li></ol><p>All these questions are based on a set of <b>common principles</b> — the 12 economic principles (4 on individual choice, 5 on interaction, 3 on economy-wide interactions).</p>",
