  "topic": "Five principles on interaction",
  "explain": "<p>Slide 33 is the professor's heading <b>\"II. Five principles on interaction\"</b> (\"Five\" in red) over a textbook table: <i>Principles That Underlie the Interaction of Individual Choices</i>.</p><p>Why \"interaction\"? Because my choices are shaped by other people's choices. When I buy, someone sells. These 5 principles explain what happens when many people's choices meet.</p><ol><li><b>There are gains from trade.</b> People get more of what they want by trading than by doing everything themselves, because each person specializes in what he or she does well.</li><li><b>Markets move toward equilibrium.</b> Things settle until no one would be better off doing something different (checkout lines).</li><li><b>Resources should be used as efficiently as possible to achieve society's goals.</b> Don't waste chances to make someone better off without hurting others.</li><li><b>Markets usually lead to efficiency.</b> Market incentives usually put resources to good use.</li><li><b>When markets don't achieve efficiency, government intervention can improve society's welfare.</b> (Side effects, monopoly, goods like drugs.)</li></ol><p>Where it fits: the 12 principles = 4 on individual choice (I) + <b>5 on interaction (II)</b> + 3 on economy-wide interactions (III).</p>",
  "question": "<p>List the five principles that underlie the interaction of individual choices, and briefly explain each one.</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The five principles on interaction are:</p><ol><li><b>There are gains from trade.</b> Through trade, people get more of what they want than if they tried to be self-sufficient. This comes from specialization: each person does the task he or she is good at.</li><li><b>Markets move toward equilibrium.</b> Equilibrium is when no individual would be better off doing something different (e.g. supermarket checkout lines become equal in length).</li><li><b>Resources should be used as efficiently as possible to achieve society's goals.</b> An economy is efficient if it takes all opportunities to make some people better off without making others worse off. (Equity = fair share, but less well-defined.)</li><li><b>Markets usually lead to efficiency.</b> The incentives built into a market economy usually put resources to good use, so chances to make people better off are not wasted.</li><li><b>When markets don't achieve efficiency, government intervention can improve society's welfare.</b> Examples: side effects not counted by the market, monopoly, goods like drugs that markets can't manage well.</li></ol>",
