  "kind": "theory",
  "topic": "Table 3-1: factors that shift demand",
  "explain": "<p>Slides 18 and 19 are one big table (Table 3-1, <i>Factors That Shift Demand</i>). Every cell has a small graph. <b>Demand increases</b> = the curve shifts <b>right</b> (D1 → D2, arrow →). <b>Demand decreases</b> = the curve shifts <b>left</b> (arrow ←).</p><p>Remember: a change in the good's <i>own</i> price is a movement <i>along</i> the curve. These factors are <i>other</i> things, so they <b>shift</b> the whole curve.</p><p><b>Slide 18 (part 1):</b></p><table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Demand increases (shift right)</th><th>Demand decreases (shift left)</th></tr><tr><td>Substitute (coffee and tea)</td><td>price of substitute <b>rises</b></td><td>price of substitute <b>falls</b></td></tr><tr><td>Complement (coffee and sugar)</td><td>price of complement <b>falls</b></td><td>price of complement <b>rises</b></td></tr><tr><td>Income, <b>normal</b> good</td><td>income <b>rises</b></td><td>income <b>falls</b></td></tr></table><p><b>Slide 19 (part 2):</b></p><table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Demand increases (shift right)</th><th>Demand decreases (shift left)</th></tr><tr><td>Income, <b>inferior</b> good</td><td>income <b>falls</b></td><td>income <b>rises</b></td></tr><tr><td>Tastes</td><td>tastes change <b>in favor of</b> the good</td><td>tastes change <b>against</b> the good</td></tr><tr><td>Expected future price</td><td>price expected to <b>rise</b> in the future → demand up <b>today</b></td><td>price expected to <b>fall</b> in the future → demand down <b>today</b></td></tr><tr><td>Number of consumers</td><td>number of consumers <b>rises</b> → <i>market</i> demand up</td><td>number of consumers <b>falls</b> → <i>market</i> demand down</td></tr></table><p>Easy way to think:</p><ul><li><b>Substitute</b> = \"instead of\". Tea gets expensive → people switch to coffee → coffee demand up. Same direction.</li><li><b>Complement</b> = \"used together\". Sugar gets expensive → people drink less sweet coffee → coffee demand down. Opposite direction.</li><li><b>Expectations</b>: if you hear phones will cost more next month, you buy one <i>today</i>.</li></ul><p>Note: slide 13 says there are 4 principal shifters (related goods' prices, income, tastes, expectations). The table adds a 5th: the number of consumers, which shifts <i>market</i> demand.</p>",
  "question": "<p>Two questions are likely from this table:</p><p><b>Q5.</b> (Slide 18) Explain how the demand curve for a good shifts when (a) the price of a substitute rises/falls, (b) the price of a complement rises/falls, (c) income rises/falls for a normal good. Draw/describe the shift.</p><p><b>Q6.</b> (Slide 19) Explain how demand shifts when (a) income rises/falls for an inferior good, (b) tastes change, (c) the price is expected to rise/fall in the future, (d) the number of consumers rises/falls.</p><p>(Or as scenarios, e.g. \"The price of tea rises. What happens to the demand for coffee?\")</p>",
  "answer": "<p>An increase in demand shifts the demand curve to the <b>right</b> (D1 → D2); a decrease shifts it to the <b>left</b>. These factors shift the whole curve (a change in the good's own price is only a movement along it).</p><p><b>Q5 (Table 3-1, part 1):</b></p><ul><li><b>Substitutes</b> (e.g. coffee and tea): when the price of a substitute <b>rises</b>, demand for the original good <b>increases</b> (shift right). When it <b>falls</b>, demand <b>decreases</b> (shift left). Example: tea price ↑ → demand for coffee ↑.</li><li><b>Complements</b> (e.g. coffee and sugar): when the price of a complement <b>falls</b>, demand for the original good <b>increases</b>. When it <b>rises</b>, demand <b>decreases</b>. Example: sugar price ↑ → demand for coffee ↓.</li><li><b>Normal good</b>: when income <b>rises</b>, demand <b>increases</b>; when income <b>falls</b>, demand <b>decreases</b>.</li></ul><p><b>Q6 (Table 3-1, part 2):</b></p><ul><li><b>Inferior good</b>: when income <b>falls</b>, demand <b>increases</b>; when income <b>rises</b>, demand <b>decreases</b>.</li><li><b>Tastes</b>: tastes change in favor of the good → demand increases; tastes change against the good → demand decreases.</li><li><b>Expectations</b>: if the price is expected to <b>rise</b> in the future, demand <b>today</b> increases; if it is expected to <b>fall</b>, demand today decreases.</li><li><b>Number of consumers</b>: more consumers → <b>market</b> demand increases; fewer consumers → market demand decreases.</li></ul>",
  "memorize": [
   "Increase in demand = shift RIGHT; decrease = shift LEFT",
   "Substitute price ↑ → demand ↑ (same direction)",
   "Complement price ↑ → demand ↓ (opposite direction)",
   "Income ↑: normal good D ↑, inferior good D ↓",
   "Price expected to rise later → demand ↑ today",
   "More consumers → market demand ↑"
  ]
