  "kind": "theory",
  "topic": "Normal goods vs inferior goods (changes in income)",
  "explain": "<p>Slide 15 is about one demand shifter: <b>changes in income</b>. It gives two definitions:</p><ul><li><b>Normal good:</b> a rise in income <b>increases</b> the demand for the good. This is \"the normal case\" — most goods are like this.</li><li><b>Inferior good:</b> a rise in income <b>decreases</b> the demand for the good.</li></ul><p>Tiny example: you are a student eating instant noodles and riding the bus. You get a good job. Now you eat in restaurants more (restaurant meals = <b>normal</b> good) and buy fewer instant noodles (noodles = <b>inferior</b> good for you). \"Inferior\" does not mean bad quality; it just means people buy less of it when they get richer.</p><p>On the graph (Table 3-1): income ↑ → normal good's demand curve shifts <b>right</b>, inferior good's demand curve shifts <b>left</b>. Income ↓ → the opposite.</p><p>Link to week 5: income elasticity of demand is <b>positive</b> for a normal good and <b>negative</b> for an inferior good.</p>",
  "question": "<p>Define a normal good and an inferior good. Give an example of each, and explain how the demand curve shifts when income rises.</p>",
  "answer": "<p>A change in income shifts the demand curve.</p><ul><li>A <b>normal good</b> is a good whose demand <b>increases</b> when income rises (the normal case). Example: restaurant meals. When income rises, its demand curve shifts to the <b>right</b>.</li><li>An <b>inferior good</b> is a good whose demand <b>decreases</b> when income rises. Example: instant noodles or bus rides — richer people buy less of them. When income rises, its demand curve shifts to the <b>left</b>.</li></ul><p>When income falls, it is the opposite: demand for a normal good falls (shift left) and demand for an inferior good rises (shift right).</p>",
