  "kind": "theory",
  "topic": "Opportunity cost (\"no such thing as a free lunch\")",
  "explain": "<p>These 4 slides are all about <b>opportunity cost</b>.</p><p><b>Slide 25:</b> \"There is no such thing as a free lunch.\" This saying is linked to the American economist <b>Milton Friedman</b>. It describes opportunity cost. <b>Every choice has at least one alternative that we passed up.</b> Even a \"free\" lunch costs you something — for example, the time you spend there.</p><p><b>Slide 26:</b> What happens when we choose? <b>Choosing one thing means we're giving up an alternative.</b> The picture is a kid at a vending machine: picking cola means giving up the juice.</p><p><b>Slide 27:</b> <b>Every activity has the opportunity cost of time.</b> This is true even for activities with <b>no money cost</b> (the picture is a hiker — hiking is free, but it takes your day). It also includes the time spent <b>searching for alternatives</b>, like looking for a new job.</p><p><b>Slide 28:</b> The definition. <b>Opportunity cost = the value of the next-best alternative.</b> For any decision, ask:</p><ul><li>How much do I value this?</li><li>What am I giving up now to have it?</li></ul><p>Opportunity cost is often expressed in <b>market prices</b> (in money).</p><p>Tiny example: on Saturday you can (a) go hiking (free), (b) work 5 hours at $8 an hour, or (c) sleep. Say working is your next-best choice. If you go hiking, your opportunity cost is the $40 you did not earn (5 × $8 = $40). So the hike is not really free. Note: you count only the <b>next-best</b> alternative, not all of them added together.</p>",
  "question": "<p>What is opportunity cost? Explain the saying \"There is no such thing as a free lunch\" and give an example. Why does even an activity with no monetary cost have an opportunity cost?</p>",
  "answer": "<p><b>Opportunity cost</b> is the value of the <b>next-best alternative</b> that we give up when we make a choice.</p><ul><li>\"There is no such thing as a free lunch\" (associated with Milton Friedman) describes opportunity cost: every choice has at least one alternative that we passed up. Choosing one thing means giving up an alternative.</li><li>Every activity has the <b>opportunity cost of time</b>. This includes activities with no monetary cost, and also the activity of searching for alternatives, like a new job.</li><li>For any decision we should ask: How much do I value this? What am I giving up now to have it?</li><li>Opportunity cost is often expressed in market prices.</li></ul><p><b>Example:</b> Hiking costs no money. But if my next-best option was working 5 hours at $8 per hour, the opportunity cost of hiking is 5 × $8 = <b>$40</b> of lost wages. So even a \"free\" activity is not free.</p>",
